{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>I will go down with you to Egypt, and I will also surely bring you up.</b> All these causes and convolutions were in order to bring them down to Egypt, to pay the debt of ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ (Genesis 15:13). And this is the deep counsel of that righteous one buried in Hebron, who counseled and who chose for him the exiles — to go down to the lands of the peoples that are deeper than the land of Canaan, in place of the depth of the netherworld of Gehinnom. And in the Yalkut it brings a midrash expounding concerning Joseph the verse (Proverbs 20:5) ״מַיִם עֲמֻקִּים עֵצָה בְלֶב אִישׁ וְאִישׁ תְּבוּנוֹת יִדְלֶנָּה״, and it appears to me to explain that Joseph too intended this — to bring about their descent to Egypt, to the place of deep waters, the waters of the insolent. And this is the counsel lodged in the heart of Joseph, and no man can fathom his mind — except Judah, a man of understanding, who plumbed the depth of his mind and drew it out to bring to light the thought he harbored concerning the Jews, to bring them down to the lands of the peoples. And therefore YHWH appeared to Jacob in visions of the night, in the manner we find that when He foretold to Abraham ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״, He appeared to him at the time the sun was setting, when a deep sleep fell upon people, as it is written (Genesis 15:12) ״וַיְהִי הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ לָבוֹא וְתַרְדֵּמָה נָפְלָה עַל אַבְרָם״ — so too YHWH appeared to Jacob in visions of the night. For all this hints at the darkness of exile, and therefore Jacob was afraid to go down to a place of darkness, cloud, and fog, until the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״אַל תִּירָא מֵרְדָה מִצְרַיְמָה כִּי לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל אֲשִׂימְךָ שָׁם״ (Genesis 46:3) — and this would come about through the affliction concerning which they said ״פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ (Exodus 1:10), while the Holy One, blessed be He, declared ״כֵּן יִרְבֶּה״; and the affliction was the cause of making them into a great nation, as it is written (Exodus 1:12) ״וְכַאֲשֶׁר יְעַנּוּ אֹתוֹ כֵּן יִרְבֶּה״." "And with this the wording that YHWH said to him is precisely accounted for: ״אָנֹכִי אֵרֵד עִמְּךָ״ — He placed the descent of the Shekhinah before his descent, for ״אָנֹכִי אֵרֵד״ means the descent of the Shekhinah; ״עִמְּךָ״ means the descent of Jacob. But concerning the ascent, the reverse is stated: ״וְאָנֹכִי אַעַלְךָ״ means the ascent of Jacob first, and afterward ״גַּם עָלֹה״ means the ascent of the Shekhinah. The parable for this: one who leads his fellow into deep waters, and his fellow is afraid to go down there lest he drown in their depth; therefore he does not go down into the water until this one leading him goes down first and he follows after him. But on the way up, he does not want his guide to go first and leave him alone in the water; rather, he goes up first and his guide after him — so that whether in the descent or in the ascent he is never left alone in the water for even a single moment. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, did not want Israel to be in exile for even a single moment without the Shekhinah; therefore in the descent He put the descent of the Shekhinah first, and in the ascent He put the ascent of Jacob first. And this is a true and precious idea." "Another matter: concerning the ascent, He mentioned the Shekhinah both before and after, for He said ״וְאָנֹכִי״ — that is the Shekhinah; ״אַעַלְךָ״ — that is the ascent of Jacob, in the middle; ״גַּם עָלֹה״ — that is the Shekhinah. But because they were in Egypt wicked and sinful, as is found in the prophecy of Ezekiel (Ezekiel 20), therefore this promise was not fulfilled in them, for the Shekhinah did not go otherwise than before them, as it is written (Exodus 13:21) ״וַה׳ הֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיהֶם״. Therefore the midrash concludes (Yalkut Shimoni Bo, 219) — as it is written ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם אֹתוֹ בְּחִפָּזוֹן״ (Exodus 12:11) — this is the haste of the Shekhinah. The meaning of the matter is: since the Shekhinah was only before them, therefore they needed to eat in haste in order to hasten their walking after the Shekhinah — like one who walks after some person leading him, he needs to walk after him quickly so as not to be distanced from the one walking before him. But in any case ״דְּבַר אֱלֹהֵינוּ יָקוּם לְעוֹלָם״ (Isaiah 40:8), and this promise will be fulfilled in the future, when they will not need to walk in haste, for they will be surrounded by the Shekhinah before and behind, as it is written (Isaiah 52:12) ״כִּי לֹא בְחִפָּזוֹן תֵּצֵאוּ וּבִמְנוּסָה לֹא תֵלֵכוּן, כִּי הֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיכֶם ה׳ וּמְאַסִּפְכֶם אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that is, before and behind. And it is the promise stated here: ״וְאָנֹכִי אַעַלְךָ גַּם עָלֹה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {0 "The phrase 'הצדיק הקבור בחברון' likely refers to Jacob himself; 'אשר ברר לו הגליות' is translated as 'who chose for him the exiles' though the syntax is somewhat strained and the antecedent of the prefixed pronoun is uncertain.", 1 "The final phrase 'וזהו ענין נכון ויקר' rendered literally as 'and this is a true and precious matter.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:56.633801721Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}